 "audit": "Cohort A: 201 cases lack a specific diagnosis and 7 cases are hypermutated (>500 rows, up to 15731), which I excluded from discovery tests; 88 cases have zero mutations; many diagnoses are tiny (<10 cases), so rates are unstable; site is largely uninformative. Protein numbering is non-canonical in places (BRAF V600E recorded as p.V640E, H3 K27M as p.K28M), which I mapped to the canonical hotspots. Cohort A looked genuine: textbook drivers (H3-3A in glioma, DDX3X/PTCH1/CTNNB1 in medulloblastoma, SMARCB1 in ATRT, BRAF in ganglioglioma/PXA, IDH1 R132H) are strongly enriched. Cohort B looked artificial or altered: it has the same diagnosis mix as A at about half the size, an empty site column, and none of the expected diagnosis-gene associations (no H3-3A in glioma, no CTNNB1/DDX3X/PTCH1 in medulloblastoma, BRAF not enriched in pilocytic astrocytoma); its top hits (CACNA1H, ZNF510, HLTF) are biologically implausible, consistent with mutation-to-case assignments having been shuffled or otherwise decoupled from diagnosis. I therefore report only two weak, low-probability claims for B.",
 "methods": "Loaded cases and mutations, counted a gene as mutated if at least one non-synonymous row existed per case. Known answers computed directly (BRAF V600E taken as p.V640E, H3 K27M as p.K28M, IDH1 any p.R132). For discovery, excluded hypermutated cases (>500 rows) and non-specific diagnoses, ran one-sided Fisher exact tests of each diagnosis vs all other cases for genes mutated in >=3 cases of the diagnosis, applied Benjamini-Hochberg, and kept hits with strong significance and biological plausibility; probabilities reflect q-value, sample size and prior plausibility, and expected frequencies were shrunk slightly toward lower values."
